WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.479
Choosing a model starts with the task.

00:00:02.479 --> 00:00:11.874
A model for casual chat may not be the right choice for coding, summarization, structured extraction, document review, or instruction following.

00:00:11.874 --> 00:00:19.443
Model size and parameter count give a rough sense of capacity, but they do not guarantee quality for every workflow.

00:00:19.443 --> 00:00:26.489
A smaller model that fits well and responds quickly may be more useful than a larger model that barely runs.

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:33.013
Quantization level determines how compact the model file is and how much RAM or VRAM it may require.

00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:41.169
More aggressive quantization can make a model usable on modest hardware, but it may reduce output quality or change behavior.

00:00:41.169 --> 00:00:48.411
The best choice depends on the task, expected quality, latency tolerance, context length, and available memory.

00:00:48.411 --> 00:00:52.847
Testing realistic prompts is better than choosing only by file size.

00:00:52.847 --> 00:00:56.175
Instruction-tuned models and chat templates matter.

00:00:56.175 --> 00:01:02.634
A chat-oriented model is usually trained to respond to role-based messages and instruction formats.

00:01:02.634 --> 00:01:09.288
If the runtime or interface uses the wrong prompt format, the model may look weaker than it really is.

00:01:09.288 --> 00:01:16.204
Context length also matters because long document workflows need more room than short single-turn prompts.

00:01:16.204 --> 00:01:22.663
Model source, provenance, and licensing should be reviewed before building workflows around a file.

00:01:22.663 --> 00:01:26.513
Prefer trusted sources and read model cards when available.

00:01:26.513 --> 00:01:31.797
Licensing may affect commercial use, redistribution, modification, or deployment.

00:01:31.797 --> 00:01:34.929
Privacy needs should also guide model selection.

00:01:34.929 --> 00:01:43.672
A local model can keep prompts on your machine, but the model file itself is still a dependency that should be tracked and documented.
